1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,880
Hi, I'm Tom from the Burp Scanner Development Team. Today I'm going to talk a little bit about

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:11,440
the difference between crawled locations and audited items. And this is because a question

3
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:17,060
we get asked quite frequently is, well, what's the difference? Specifically, if I hop into Burp

4
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:22,020
here where I've got a finished crawl and audit of Portswiggers' Honorable Web Application Gin

5
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:27,680
and Juice Shop, we can see that we've got 47 locations crawled. But if I hop over to the

6
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:32,000
audit items view, you can see that there's 50 none. So why is this? What's the difference?

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,940
Well, what it comes down to is the fact that when the crawl is running, we are crawling the web

8
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:45,860
application like a user and we treat distinct web pages as locations. Whereas when the audit is

9
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:51,320
running, we are auditing HTTP messages. So there's a bit of a difference between a HTTP message and a

10
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:56,240
web page, which I'll go over now. To do this, I'm going to use the new crawl paths view, which came

11
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:03,120
out in 2023.5. My view here might differ a little bit from your view if you open up Burp now. That's

12
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:08,240
because I'm running an internal build and we're actually constantly, like the guys behind me

13
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:16,240
somewhere there, are constantly working on this right now. So if you see any differences, just treat

14
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:23,440
them as sneak previews and don't worry about them too much. So as I said, yeah, I've got Gin and Juice

15
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:29,360
shop open and we can see all of the actions that the crawler has taken. So for example, we clicked on

16
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:34,800
new details here for one of the products. And then we see on that product page, we've got a form that

17
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:39,520
we submitted for checking the stock. We've got an add to cart form. So this is where the difference

18
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:45,600
between crawled locations and ordered items comes in. When we submitted the form add to cart, we got to a

19
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:51,600
new page in the web application. That web page had this destination URL here, but there are actually two

20
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:57,760
bits of HTTP traffic required to build the page. One to fetch the product page, which actually matches

21
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:03,520
the destination URL of the page. Now this would actually be the URL in the address bar in the

22
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:08,480
browser. That's where we get the URL from for a page. There's also the second request, which is a

23
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:14,640
post request to go and... Oh, I've clicked on add to cart. I think I was saying check, stop, but no mind.

24
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:22,160
So yeah, add cart. So we've got this request here to go and add the product to the cart, which is a

25
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:26,560
post request. So this is where in this case, we would have one crawled location, but two

26
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:31,680
ordered items for each of these requests. This can actually go the other way as well. So we've got this

27
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:37,440
subscribe form on the homepage, which when you click on it has single page app behavior where some

28
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:43,280
JavaScript runs, but there are no HP messages sent at all. So in this case, we would have a crawl

29
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:49,600
location, but no audit items. So this is how over time we can build up a difference in that number

30
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:54,080
because we're constantly crawling web pages as locations. So if I actually hop into Gin and Juke

31
00:02:54,080 --> 00:03:00,720
Shop, I can show this. So this would be a location. If we click on a view details, that would be a

32
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:06,080
location. Yeah, these are all distinct locations with their URLs coming from the address bar. Whereas

33
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:13,120
the, as I said, the requests that are issued to build these pages, these locations are what are sent to

34
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:18,720
the audit. So hopefully that clears things up a little bit. And this is also why, for example,

35
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:25,120
if you know of some addresses that are requests, some, some addresses that are navigated to in your

36
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:29,840
application, they might not appear in the audit items view, because if you've got a fully single

37
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:34,400
page application, you might be browsing around the address bar might be changing, but we're actually

38
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:38,400
not generating any requests. So there's nothing to audit or the requests that are generated might be

39
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:44,720
API requests. So the URLs, their paths in this audit items view here might not match what you're

40
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:51,840
expecting to see in the address bar. So as I said, we're actually still constantly working on this

41
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:57,040
crawl paths view. This is based on user feedback and there's plenty of goodies we've internally got

42
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:02,160
lined up to add plenty of new features. So yeah, keep that feedback coming and stay tuned.

